\title{\sys{}: Preventing Semantic Rollback Attacks in Agent Checkpoint-Restore}

\author{Yusheng Zheng\textsuperscript{1}, Yiwei Yang\textsuperscript{1}, Wei Zhang\textsuperscript{2}, Andi Quinn\textsuperscript{1} \\[4pt]
{\normalfont\small \textsuperscript{1}UC Santa Cruz \quad \textsuperscript{2}University of Connecticut \\
\texttt{\{yzhen165, yyang363, aquinn1\}@ucsc.edu, wei.13.zhang@uconn.edu}}}
\affiliation{\institution{}\country{}}

\begin{abstract}
	Checkpoint-restore mechanisms, increasingly adopted in LLM agent frameworks for both error recovery and exploration, reintroduce a classic systems challenge: externally visible outputs cannot be rolled back. Unlike traditional deterministic systems, tool-using LLM agents may depend on implicit execution context (e.g., user permissions in prompt, quotas, external API state), and re-synthesize semantically equivalent but subtly different actions after restoring from checkpoints. These subtly different re-generated calls silently bypass duplicate-detection mechanisms and may revive consumed credentials, leading to what we term \emph{semantic rollback attacks}. We characterize two vulnerability classes, \emph{Action Replay} and \emph{Authority Resurrection}, and demonstrate their reproducibility in a proof-of-concept testbed with realistic external tool servers. We introduce \sys{} (Agent Checkpoint-Restore Fence), a framework-agnostic runtime mechanism that logs every irreversible external tool effect and enforces safe replay-or-fork semantics across execution branches. \sys{} captures tool-call context as stable effect fingerprints via eBPF and reliably distinguishes replay from exploration upon restoration. We demonstrate \sys{} can eliminate semantic rollback vulnerabilities while preserving agents' flexibility for exploration of alternative execution paths.
\end{abstract}

