\section{Background and Motivation}

\textbf{Checkpoint-restore (CR).} Checkpoint-restore (CR) systems such as CRIU and Docker Checkpoint save and restore local execution states (memory, file descriptors, and container-local filesystems~\cite{criu,mvvm}), but they cannot undo actions already performed on external resources (remote APIs, databases, network services). After restoration, external states previously altered by agent actions remain irreversibly modified~\cite{elnozahy2002survey, strom1985optimistic}. CR is increasingly employed not only for fault recovery but also for exploring alternative execution paths~\cite{xu2025agenticexploration}, yet mainstream agent frameworks provide only conversation-level checkpoints without consistency guarantees for external operations.

\textbf{LLM Agents real world CR issue.} LLM agents manage cloud resources, payments, and software engineering tasks~\cite{sweagent, openhands}. Agent frameworks expose persistent checkpoint-restore as a first-class feature~\cite{langgraph, langgraph_durable}; their guidance warns that resumed execution may rerun side-effecting steps and recommends that tool calls be made safe to retry~\cite{langgraph_functional_idempotency}. Existing duplicate-detection mechanisms (e.g., attaching a unique request ID so the server can reject repeats~\cite{stripe_idempotent_requests, ietf_idempotency_key}) all assume the caller will send \emph{identical} requests on retry. LLM agents break this assumption: after restore, they do not deterministically re-execute the same call but \emph{re-synthesize} a semantically equivalent call with different parameters, even under temperature=0~\cite{atil2025nondeterminism, khatchadourian2026replayable}. The server therefore treats the retried call as a brand-new request. Durable engines like Temporal record nondeterministic values for replay~\cite{temporal_side_effects}, but require the caller's control flow to be deterministic, which LLM agents cannot guarantee.  A Vault issue documents single-use tokens reappearing after snapshot restore~\cite{vault_issue_28378}, illustrating the problem in practice.

\textbf{Related Work.} Effect journaling for safe replay has precedents in I/O tabling~\cite{somogyi2003iotabling} and durable execution engines~\cite{birrell1984rpc, temporal_side_effects}; together with duplicate-detection protocols~\cite{stripe_idempotent_requests, ietf_idempotency_key}, these all assume deterministic caller behavior, as discussed above. Agent record-and-replay improves reliability~\cite{feng2025agentrR} and version-control systems provide rollback and branching for agent traces~\cite{li2025agentgit}, but neither addresses irreversible external effects during replay. Security mechanisms enforce privilege control for agents~\cite{progent, agentspec, owaspllm} without considering checkpoint-restore interactions. TEE rollback protection~\cite{parno2011memoir, matetic2017rote} assumes deterministic programs. No existing work treats non-deterministic LLM re-synthesis after restore as a security attack surface or provides framework-agnostic protection for irreversible tool effects.

\section{Threat Model}

We target two invariants: (1) no replay of irreversible effects across restores, and (2) consumed credentials must stay consumed after restore. We consider two attacker models: \emph{Deliberate Rollback Abuse}, where an adversary exploits CR features exposed for exploration~\cite{langgraph}, analogous to TEE restart attacks~\cite{matetic2017rote}; and \emph{Crash-Induced Restore}, where crashes trigger restoration and the attacker benefits from duplicate effects. Both require that external operations remain consistent across checkpoints.

\section{Attacks}

We identify two attack classes exploiting state divergence after restore. \emph{Action Replay} exploits the fact that the LLM generates different request identifiers after restore, causing the server to accept what is logically the same operation twice (e.g., duplicate payments); it applies to both attacker models. \emph{Authority Resurrection} exploits consumed single-use tokens (credentials valid for exactly one operation) that reappear in rolled-back agent state, enabling unauthorized operations; it requires Deliberate Rollback Abuse where the attacker controls post-restore prompts.

