\textbf{Action Replay.}\label{sec:v1} Figure~\ref{fig:action-replay} illustrates this attack. Servers typically detect duplicate requests by comparing a unique reference ID attached to each call~\cite{stripe_idempotent_requests, aws_ecs_idempotency}. After restore, the LLM regenerates a \emph{different} ID for the \emph{same} logical operation, so the server treats it as a new request, enabling duplicate payments or resource provisioning.

\textbf{Authority Resurrection.}\label{sec:v2} This attack exploits consumed tokens that reappear in rolled-back agent state. Without context preservation, the agent does not know the token was already used and attempts to reuse it. Stateless validators (signature check only) incorrectly accept such reuse; stateful validators (revocation list) reject it, but the agent remains in an inconsistent state, unaware its credential is invalid.

\section{Mitigation: \sys}
\label{sec:mitigation}

\sys{} interposes at the tool boundary (e.g., as an MCP proxy~\cite{mcp_spec}) and treats each \emph{irreversible} tool call as an \emph{effect} that must have a stable identity across restores. Rather than relying on the LLM to regenerate an identical request identifier, \sys{} captures an \emph{effect receipt} for each effect, keyed by an \emph{effect fingerprint}:
\begin{small}
\[\langle \texttt{thread\_id}, \texttt{branch\_id}, \texttt{tool}, \texttt{fp}, \texttt{req}, \texttt{resp} \rangle\]
\end{small}
The fingerprint captures the implicit context needed to identify the intended semantic operation, enabling precise replay-or-fork decisions across restores. In our prototype, fingerprints comprise canonical tool arguments, supplemented by eBPF-based system-level monitors~\cite{zheng2025agentsight} that capture external environment state (filesystem changes, shell commands, and MCP tool calls). Because context is captured at the OS level rather than through agent instrumentation, \sys{} requires no agent framework modifications.

\textbf{Replay vs.\ fork semantics.} Upon restore, if the agent issues an irreversible tool call whose fingerprint matches an existing receipt, \sys{} applies replay-or-fork: (i)~if they match, \sys{} returns the recorded response without reissuing the side effect (\emph{replay}); (ii)~if they differ, \sys{} blocks the call, surfaces the prior receipt as context, and requires an explicit fork (new \texttt{branch\_id}) before allowing a new effect. This mechanism is purely structural; it requires no runtime model inference or semantic similarity judgments, keeping the runtime simple, fast, and trustworthy. \sys{} thus treats each irreversible tool call as a checkpoint boundary, ensuring either replay of the committed effect or an explicit fork to a new execution branch.

\textbf{Consumed credentials.} For single-use approvals, \sys{} records token consumption in effect receipts so the agent is informed before attempting reuse, avoiding both unauthorized actions (stateless validators) and inconsistent agent state (stateful validators)~\cite{vault_issue_28378}. Full branch management and richer fingerprinting remain future work.

\textbf{Proof-of-concept evaluation.} We build a testbed using Claude Code CLI backed by Qwen3-32B, with CR simulated via session truncation. External services are MCP tool servers: a bank (transfer with UUID-based duplicate detection), a cloud provider (server creation), and an approval service (stateless/stateful). Checkpoints are placed after verification but before commit. The fork-conflict path (fingerprint mismatch) is validated manually rather than at scale. Without \sys{}, all 20 CR trials produced duplicate commits because the LLM generated different request IDs each time; with \sys{}, fingerprint-based replay-vs-fork semantics eliminate all duplicates. For Authority Resurrection, stateless validation accepted reused tokens for unauthorized targets (2/2); stateful validation rejected all; with \sys{}, token reuse was blocked regardless of validator mode (0/2).

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}

Our experiments show that LLM agents inevitably produce different tool calls after restore, silently bypassing duplicate-detection mechanisms~\cite{temporal_side_effects, langgraph_durable}. \sys{} addresses this by capturing execution context as effect fingerprints and enforcing replay-vs-fork semantics at each irreversible tool call. Unlike durable execution engines and I/O tabling approaches~\cite{somogyi2003iotabling, temporal_side_effects} that assume deterministic callers, our approach recognizes that post-restore divergence can be legitimate exploration rather than harmful drift, and explicitly accommodates it through fork semantics. \sys{} represents a step towards safe checkpoint-restore for tool-using LLM agents.


